course: dictionary
lesson: 12511
title: Derisive: the sneering word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Derisive means showing that you think someone or something is stupid or worthless, so you mock them. Context clues help you spot it. Look for words like laugh, snort and comments, and ask who is being laughed at.
explain: «She gave a derisive laugh when he missed the goal.» The laugh is not a happy one. It tells you she thinks he is useless and wants him to feel small. Writers use derisive to show that a character is unkind or scornful.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «mocking» in place of derisive: «She gave a mocking laugh when he missed the goal.» It still makes sense, so mocking is a good clue. Derisive is the more formal word, and it sounds stronger.
fact: Derisive means showing that you think someone or something is stupid or worthless; mocking.
fact: «A derisive laugh» is a laugh that mocks someone.
fact: Derisive is often used about bullies and villains in stories, to show they are unkind.
fact: A less common meaning is deserving to be laughed at, or ridiculous; «derisory» is usually the better word for that.
fact: Derisive is an adjective: derisive, more derisive, most derisive.
exercise: «The crowd let out a derisive laugh when he tripped.» What does derisive show? | That they are very proud of him | *That they think he is ridiculous and mocking | That they are cold and sleepy | That they are about to cry
exercise: «Her derisive comments made the new girl feel small.» Find and copy the word that tells you the comments were unkind. | *derisive | comments | feel | new
exercise: The villain gave a derisive laugh. What kind of character is he? | *Unkind and scornful | Very tired | Lost and frightened | Kind and gentle
test: «Everyone heard his derisive laugh across the playground.» What does derisive mean? | Loud and cheerful | Showing that you are very happy | Quiet and shy | *Showing that you think someone is worthless
test: Which phrase best fits a bully in a story? | a warm hug | *a derisive snort | a gentle smile | a quiet nap
exam: Which sentence shows a derisive comment? | «I love the sound of the rain.» | «Well done, what a clever idea!» | *«Oh, that drawing is so silly, who would ever look at it?» | «Could you pass the salt, please?»
